•Proposition:A declarative sentence that is either true or false, never both.
•Connectives: negation ¬p, conjunction p ∧ q, disjunction p ∨ q, exclusive or p ⊕ q, conditional p → q, biconditional p ↔ q.
•Tautology: The last column of truth table always true. Contradiction: The last column of truth table  always false. Contingency: sometimes true, sometimes false
•Logical equivalence (≡): two compound propositions have the same truth value in every row.
•Conditional forms for p → q: converse q → p, inverse ¬p → ¬q, contrapositive ¬q → ¬p.
•Predicate: a statement P(x) with a variable; it becomes a proposition when x is given a value or quantified.
•Universal quantifier ∀x P(x): P(x) is true for every x in the domain. 
•Existential quantifier ∃x P(x): true for at least one x.
•Statement is p → q and q follows from p by algebra or definitions: direct.
•Direct gets messy but ¬q gives a clean handle on ¬p: contraposition.
•Statement says something does not exist or is irrational or impossible: contradiction.
•Statement is about many types (even/odd, positive/negative): cases.
•Statement is "for all" and you suspect it is false: look for a counterexample.
•Statement is p ↔ q: prove both directions.
