Verbal communication
•Definition: it’s exchanging information through spoken words, face to face, on a call, or in a meeting or presentation.
•Forms: oral (speaking and listening) and written (emails, reports) are the two verbal types. Most people mean the spoken one.
•Key elements: sender, message, channel, receiver, feedback. Noise (distractions, accent barriers, bad network) can block the message.
Advantages:
•It’s fast.
•You get instant feedback.
•Tone and emotion carry the meaning.
•It builds personal rapport.
Disadvantages:
•There’s no permanent record.
•Words can be misheard or misunderstood.
•Spoken messages are less carefully planned than written ones.
Qualities of good verbal communication: the 7 Cs are clear, concise, correct, complete, concrete, courteous and coherent.
•Voice factors: volume, pitch, pace, pauses and tone change how the same words land.
•Active listening: pay full attention, don’t interrupt, and paraphrase to confirm you understood.
Barriers:
•Language and accent differences.
•Jargon.
•Poor listening.
•Emotions.
•Noise.
•Cultural differences.
Verbal vs non-verbal: verbal uses words. Non-verbal uses body language, eye contact, gestures and facial expressions. The two work best together.
Examples: interviews, group discussions, presentations, phone calls, team meetings.
Tips to improve:
•Speak slowly and clearly.
•Keep sentences short.
•Avoid filler words (“um”, “like”).
•Practise out loud.
•Ask questions to check understanding.
